export const narration = {
  try:{title:'01 · Try the partnership',text:'Imagine you are planning a small event. Your budget is open in QindaCalc, and you ask: try two lighting packages, and tell me if we still fit. In this proposed experience, the partner reads the active sheet, the selected cell, the formulas, and the current document revision. It changes the lighting quantity through the application’s editing code. Calc recalculates. The partner then explains the verified result: nine thousand, eight hundred and eighty-two dollars in planned costs, twelve hundred and eighty-two above the ceiling. You see the change, and you can undo it. Try the Run button, switch between the built-in assistant and the command line, then inspect the structured view below. Both entry points use the same document state. The browser demonstration uses fictional sample data. It is not connected to your desktop. The important idea is that the application explains its meaning and available operations to the agent, and verifies what happened.'},
  read:{title:'02 · What the agent sees',text:'The screen is only one view of your work. A dependable partner needs to know which document is active, which sheet or page you are looking at, what is selected, and whether you are still typing a value. It also needs exact values, formulas, units, errors, filters, and any dialog that blocks an action. A stable identifier lets it address a cell or slide even after the window moves. A revision tells it whether the document has changed since it read the state. An action description explains what the application can do and which inputs it expects. A receipt tells the agent which targets changed and what the new values are. Accessibility is a useful foundation for controls and semantic views. Document operations add the meaning needed for office work. Images, drawings, and visual appearance may still benefit from screenshots or vision. But routine edits and questions about exact values can use the application’s own structured information.'},
  today:{title:'03 · Your office today',text:'Your office suite is already partway there. QindaOffice has an assistant in each document window. It can provide selection or document context to a model, including models reached through Claude, Codex, and Kimi command line providers, or compatible web endpoints. Its document tools describe the document, explain the available edits, apply them, and support undo. Calc can change cells. Write can replace text. Show can edit slides and speaker notes. Note can edit page blocks. Window actions also have stable identifiers and enabled state. The live notes pipeline already uses structured Jev-style decisions. What is missing is one consistent live contract that an outside agent can discover and use. The photographs in this section are real application screenshots, and the film shows real edits from the existing suite. They are evidence of the foundation, not evidence that the proposed shared bridge has already been implemented.'},
  access:{title:'04 · Built-in or terminal',text:'There are two useful ways to work with an AI partner. Inside the application, you select something and ask the built-in assistant for help. From a terminal, an external agent could connect to a document you have granted it access to, inspect the current workflow, and perform the same edit. The core service should be shared, so permissions, validation, and undo behave consistently. A local command line interface can print structured results for scripts. An MCP adapter can expose tools and resources to compatible agent clients. An in-process API can serve the embedded assistant. These are different doors into the same application-owned contract. The commands shown here are proposed examples, not commands that are already installed. Also, the suite’s current support for a model’s command line provider is not itself an external document-control API. It runs the model for the assistant. Building the bridge would let another agent reach the application’s live state and actions directly.'},
  workflow:{title:'05 · A day together',text:'Now imagine the whole planning day. During a meeting, QindaNote captures what was decided, which tasks remain, and what is still uncertain. You review those notes. The partner then uses an accepted decision to update the approval paragraph in QindaWrite. In Calc, it tests the lighting assumption and reads the recalculated budget. In Show, it puts the verified caveat and approval reminder into your speaker notes. Click through the four handoffs to see how the human experience could feel. The value is keeping source context and making changes traceable, rather than copying everything by hand. This cross-application journey is a future design. Today, each app already has useful document operations, but coordinated access and provenance still need work. Each document should report its own result and undo. If the partner cannot read a source, or a note lacks the amount needed for a budget edit, it should ask you instead of inventing the missing fact.'},
  architecture:{title:'06 · How it connects',text:'The proposed toolkit service is called Agent Host in this guide. That name is a design suggestion, not a class that already exists. It lives with the application and collects structured state, action descriptions, permissions, revisions, and task events. Adapters connect it to the toolkit’s accessibility and command machinery, and to the office suite’s existing document tools. The native interface remains your interface. The embedded assistant, a local command line, and an MCP client can all use the shared service. Click a diagram node to explore its responsibility. Models have different jobs here. A larger model can understand language, plan a task, and explain an outcome. Jev can make a focused decision among explicit choices or decide when a larger call is worthwhile. The application remains the authority for validation, calculations, editing, and undo. A model’s confidence is useful input; it does not prove that the document was changed.'},
  control:{title:'07 · You stay in control',text:'A good partner expects you to keep working. Suppose it reads revision twelve and proposes a change, but you edit the same document before it applies that proposal. The application should detect the stale revision, preserve your new work, and require a refreshed view. Try that situation in this section. You can also try read-only access, an invalid value, and a retried request. These demonstrations illustrate proposed guarantees for the shared contract. Read-only access should not become edit permission. Invalid values should fail before changing the document. A repeated request identifier should return the earlier receipt rather than apply the edit twice. Activity should be visible, and long tasks should have a Stop control. Ordinary document edits use the document’s undo stack. External effects need their own rules. Text inside a document is content to analyze, not a source of new authority. The application enforces those boundaries even when the model proposes something else.'},
  roadmap:{title:'08 · The path from here',text:'This project has a substantial head start. The office suite already owns document snapshots, edit operations, and undo, while QindaTK already has accessibility, semantic views, commands, selection, validation, and revision machinery. The first useful slice could be one open Calc document: read the active budget, change a cell, recalculate, explain the result, and undo, from both an embedded client and a local command line. For one experienced Qt developer, one to two weeks is a rough estimate for that slice. A reusable Linux service with typed schemas, revisions, events, permissions, and CLI and MCP adapters could take roughly four to eight weeks in total. Complex views, formatting fidelity, cross-application coordination, and other platforms add further months depending on scope. These are planning estimates, not measured delivery dates. The best first move is to prove the budget experience end to end, then lift the shared bridge into the toolkit and connect the other office apps through the editing seams they already have.'},
};
